<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catalan Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1462">1462</a>–<a href="/years/1472">1472</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Catalan Civil War was fought from 1462 to 1472 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> and the Principality of Catalonia, whose institutions rejected his rule and offered the crown to several rival princes. The remença peasants supported the king. John II eventually took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1472 and confirmed Catalan privileges, but the long war left Catalonia economically ruined.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The quarrel began within the royal family. John II, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> through his first wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blanche-i-of-navarre">Blanche I of Navarre</a>, kept her lands after her death in 1441 and dispossessed their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-prince-of-viana">Charles, Prince of Viana</a>; father and son fought openly in Navarre in the early 1450s. On succeeding his brother Alfonso V in 1458, John refused to recognise Charles as his first-born, probably to keep that title for Ferdinand, his son by Juana Enríquez. When he had Charles arrested at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a> on 2 December 1460, Catalonia rose in protest.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-courts">Catalan Courts</a> ruled that the king had broken the Usatges de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and the Constitucions de Catalunya, and an army under the Count of Modica forced him to give way. By the Capitulation of Vilafranca of 21 June 1461 Charles was recognised as heir, and the king could no longer enter the principality without the permission of the Generalitat. Charles died of tuberculosis in Barcelona on 23 September 1461, and the rivalry of the Busca and the Biga factions revived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Fighting opened in February 1462 with the rising of the remença peasants under Francesc de Verntallat. By the treaties of Sauveterre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a> in May 1462, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI of France</a> lent John 700 lances in return for 200,000 écus, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdagne</a> as surety. The Consell del Principat deposed the king, and the Catalans offered the crown in turn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-castile">Henry IV of Castile</a>, who renounced it on 13 June 1463; to the constable of Portugal, acclaimed as Peter V, who was defeated at the Battle of Calaf on 28 February 1465 and died in 1466; and to René the Good, Count of Anjou and Provence.</p><p>Supplies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> sustained the king. René's son John II, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, defeated Prince Ferdinand at the Battle of Viladamat on 21 November 1467, but the duke's death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> on 16 December 1470 and the withdrawal of French troops in 1471 turned the war. Barcelona, besieged by land and sea from 1 January 1472, surrendered on 16 October by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulation-of-pedralbes">Capitulation of Pedralbes</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The settlement granted a general pardon, though not to Hug Roger III, Count of Pallars Sobirà, and approved the acts of the Catalan government since the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-prince-of-viana">Charles of Viana</a>, while the Capitulation of Vilafranca was rejected. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdagne</a> proved harder to recover: the French conquered Roussillon in 1474, and in October 1478 John ceded both counties to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> until he could redeem them. The Catalan historians Santiago Sobrequés and Jaume Sobrequés saw in the rebels' cause a new idea of a community bound by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common laws</a>, and the French historian Joseph Calmette called the rising the first of modern revolutions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Catalan Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
